WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.520
The first film showed oxygen changing what VHL could recognize.

2
00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:10.100
Why does one ubiquitin mark move a protein while another destroys it?

3
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.860
Keep the same colors: yellow ubiquitin, grey targets, green E3 and red proteasome.

4
00:00:20.283 --> 00:00:23.223
Start with one ubiquitin attached to one lysine.

5
00:00:24.463 --> 00:00:28.523
This monoubiquitin mark seldom sends a protein to the proteasome.

6
00:00:29.763 --> 00:00:31.483
It more often changes location.

7
00:00:32.863 --> 00:00:37.583
On a growth-factor receptor, readers can help pull the receptor into the cell by

8
00:00:37.583 --> 00:00:38.583
endocytosis.

9
00:00:39.583 --> 00:00:44.323
Now reconnect the yellow blocks into the classic proteasome-directed linkage.

10
00:00:45.663 --> 00:00:49.203
A sufficiently long chain is a usual proteasome signal.

11
00:00:50.363 --> 00:00:53.883
The red barrel engages the marked protein and breaks it down.

12
00:00:55.143 --> 00:00:57.143
This is the classic destruction route.

13
00:00:58.167 --> 00:01:02.847
Reconnect the same yellow blocks through another linkage, and the picture changes

14
00:01:02.847 --> 00:01:07.167
again. Ubiquitin-binding domains clamp onto this chain.

15
00:01:08.527 --> 00:01:11.407
Several proteins can gather around one scaffold.

16
00:01:12.607 --> 00:01:18.107
Signaling complexes and repair proteins at damaged DNA can assemble this way.

17
00:01:19.117 --> 00:01:23.117
Return to a receptor carrying only one yellow ubiquitin.

18
00:01:24.257 --> 00:01:27.777
Is that single mark enough to commit the receptor to the proteasome?

19
00:01:29.237 --> 00:01:31.577
Choose a destination before the picture moves.

20
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:43.900
Not usually. In this example, the single mark helps route the receptor into a

21
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:50.400
vesicle. Movement and destruction can still cooperate later, because internalized

22
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.920
receptors are often carried to lysosomes.

23
00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.780
The mark suggests an outcome; it does not guarantee one alone.

24
00:01:58.883 --> 00:02:02.303
So ubiquitin behaves as a context-dependent code.

25
00:02:03.683 --> 00:02:08.163
Linkage, chain length, branching, substrate and available readers all matter.

26
00:02:09.483 --> 00:02:12.163
A simple lookup table would show too much certainty.

27
00:02:13.463 --> 00:02:15.983
Keep the mark, its reader and the target together.

28
00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:21.933
A signal can also change recognition without modifying the target first.

29
00:02:23.273 --> 00:02:26.413
In plants, TIR1 is a green E3 receptor.

30
00:02:27.753 --> 00:02:33.053
An Aux/IAA repressor rests nearby, but their contact is weak without the hormone

31
00:02:33.053 --> 00:02:34.053
auxin.

32
00:02:34.567 --> 00:02:39.767
Auxin fits into a pocket exactly where TIR1 and the repressor meet.

33
00:02:41.007 --> 00:02:43.067
The small gold signal fills their gap.

34
00:02:44.287 --> 00:02:46.947
Their grip tightens without building a separate cascade.

35
00:02:48.507 --> 00:02:52.287
Auxin acts as molecular glue at the target-receptor interface.

36
00:02:53.417 --> 00:02:58.517
The ligase now ubiquitinates the repressor, and the proteasome removes it.

37
00:02:59.697 --> 00:03:02.437
Auxin-responsive genes are released from repression.

38
00:03:03.897 --> 00:03:08.257
Here hormone detection and target recruitment happen in one binding step.

39
00:03:09.477 --> 00:03:12.817
The signal triggers destruction by tightening an E3 grip.

40
00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:15.790
These two ideas work together.

41
00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:22.110
The E3 chooses a recognized target, while the ubiquitin mark recruits particular

42
00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:28.630
readers. Ubiquitin marks route proteins; molecular glues change what an E3 grips.

43
00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:32.610
Follow both questions to predict a target's fate.
